Experiment, not a proposed fix. Disables the stride check that rejects the
libstdc++ (manually 4x-unrolled) shape in LoopVectorizationLegality.

Result: the loop passes legality ("LV: We can vectorize this loop!") and then
bails at the *same* VPlan gate as the plain shape:
    LV: Not vectorizing: Auto-vectorization of loops with potentially faulting
        load is not supported.
Zero vector.body blocks either way. Relaxing this check alone is a no-op.

Apply with:  git apply exp-relax-stride.patch && ninja -C ../../build-debug opt
Revert with: git apply -R exp-relax-stride.patch && ninja -C ../../build-debug opt

diff --git a/llvm/lib/Transforms/Vectorize/LoopVectorizationLegality.cpp b/llvm/lib/Transforms/Vectorize/LoopVectorizationLegality.cpp
--- a/llvm/lib/Transforms/Vectorize/LoopVectorizationLegality.cpp
+++ b/llvm/lib/Transforms/Vectorize/LoopVectorizationLegality.cpp
@@ -1751,7 +1751,7 @@
   // Check non-dereferenceable loads if any.
   for (LoadInst *LI : NonDerefLoads) {
     // Only support unit-stride access for now.
     int Stride = isConsecutivePtr(LI->getType(), LI->getPointerOperand());
-    if (Stride != 1) {
+    if (false && Stride != 1) { // XXX-206006-EXPERIMENT: accept any stride
       reportVectorizationFailure(
           "Loop contains potentially faulting strided load",
           "Cannot vectorize early exit loop with "
